# xnip — Cursor Rule

Use `xnip` CLI for any non-trivial file edit. It compresses "read-then-replace" into "path + locator + new bytes", saving ≥ 70% tokens on line-level ops.

## Decision rules

- Need to **show specific lines** to the user → `xnip peek <file> --lines a-b`
- Need to **find** something → `xnip find <files...> --pattern '<regex>'`
- Need to **edit a known line range** → `xnip replace <file> --lines a-b --text '...'`
- Need to **edit by anchor** → `xnip replace <file> --match-line '^const PORT' --text '...'`
- Need to **edit a block bounded by markers** → `xnip replace <file> --between-re '^fn foo' '^}' --inclusive --text-file ./snippet.txt`
- Need to **rename across a file** → `xnip replace <file> --pattern '\bOLD\b' --repl NEW`
- Need to **insert / move / re-indent** → `xnip insert | xnip move | xnip indent`
- Need to **apply many edits atomically** → `xnip apply edits.txt`

## Locators

- `--lines 30` / `--lines 30-45`
- `--match-line '<regex>' [--occurrence N]`
- `--between 'BEGIN'..'END' [--inclusive]`
- `--between-re '^fn foo'..'^}' [--inclusive]`
- `--pattern '<regex>'` (replace only; combine with `--repl`, `--count`)

## Content

- `--text "literal\n"` (literal, supports `\n`)
- `--text-stdin` (read from stdin)
- `--text-file <path>` (read from file)
- `--text ""` to delete

## Safety

- `--was "expected\n"` / `--was-file` — guard against drift
- `--dry-run` — preview new content (or unified diff for apply)
- `--check` — validate without writing (exit 3 on failure)
- `--backup` — write `<file>.bak` before atomic rename (default off)

## apply (preferred for batch)

```sh
xnip apply edits.txt          # native (auto)
xnip apply edits.json         # JSON
xnip apply edits.yaml         # YAML
xnip apply edits.txt --dry-run
xnip apply edits.txt --check
xnip apply edits.txt --json   # NDJSON event stream
```

JSON schema:

```json
[
  {"op":"replace","file":"a.txt","lines":"30","text":"X"},
  {"op":"replace","file":"a.txt","pattern":"OLD","repl":"NEW","count":"all"},
  {"op":"insert","file":"a.txt","lines":5,"where":"after","text":"X"},
  {"op":"move","file":"a.txt","lines":"10-20","to":100},
  {"op":"indent","file":"a.txt","lines":"30-45","by":2}
]
```

## Exit codes

`0` success · `1` user error · `2` IO · `3` validation · `4` apply partial commit

## MCP server (preferred when available)

Cursor supports [Model Context Protocol](https://modelcontextprotocol.io/) servers. If `xnip mcp` is available, register it via `.cursor/mcp.json` (project) or `~/.cursor/mcp.json` (global):

```json
{
  "mcpServers": {
    "xnip": { "command": "xnip", "args": ["mcp"] }
  }
}
```

You will see 8 tools (`xnip_peek` / `xnip_find` / `xnip_replace` / `xnip_insert` / `xnip_move` / `xnip_indent` / `xnip_apply` / `xnip_doctor`) appear in the tool palette—prefer them over shelling out to the cli when both are available, since structured arguments avoid quoting / escaping pitfalls.
